  "narrative": {
    "top_frame": "They trailed after four, took the lead with seven runs in the fifth, and never let it back.",
    "what_this_means": "This is what controlled offense looks like: patient enough to work counts, aggressive enough to punish mistakes. The Angels gave up seven in one inning because the Athletics found something to swing at and did damage. Ginn gave them six solid innings. Three earned runs on eight hits, five strikeouts. And the bullpen didn't blink. Medina struck out three in 1.1 scoreless. Harris and Alvarado followed without damage. The lineup converted five of eleven RISP situations. That is the exact ratio a team needs when margin is small and division pressure is real. Two-run games are won by teams that can do both at once: get runners on and drive them in. They did. Once you score seven in one inning, you own the day.",
